    docs(latte): H3-1 — blocked kernel-gate fixture diagnostic packet
    
    Diagnoses the two blocked fixtures of the slice-9 kernel differential gate (check-accepted and check-assume-have-discharge) across both C evaluators (walker/semantic) at 2M, 4M, and 8M instruction budgets, with zero production or acceptance-budget changes.
    
    Key findings (docs/slice9-h3-1-blocked-fixtures.md):
    - Neither fixture loops infinitely: both halt deterministically and agree with A and B (check-accepted at 1,207,270 semantic / 2,086,120 walker steps; check-assume-have-discharge at 5,030,283 semantic / ~8.69M projected walker steps, reaching 2,219/2,360 calls = 94.03% at 8M).
    - Primary root cause (95.1%-95.7% of execution steps): §8.10 common-call + outcome per-call lowering amplification in yang.clojure (36 -> 538 semantic steps/call = 14.94x; 65 -> 934 walker steps/call = 14.37x). Actual LaTTe kernel bodies take 32,700 semantic instructions (0.65%).
    - Secondary root cause (check-accepted lane split): 1.7280x CESK tree-walker vs register-VM step granularity on identical UAST.
    - Observer audit: run-bounded resets n=0 per run call, but kernel-c queues a single batch (:run-calls 1); setup is 1.04%-1.79% of 2M; run-bounded omits :consumer v on exhaustion so consumer-left-by-run falls back to step 0.
    - Also tracks test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_declarations.edn omitted from git add in H3-0 (1fd7236be), verified via test-kernel-acceptance-counting and test-kernel-gate-evidence-writer-round-trip.
    
    Recommends a compiler-fix round (plus 1-line harness :consumer fix) rather than raising the 2M gate budget, with full contingent budget-proposal measurements included for architect review. Focused gate pre/post: 1 test, 661 assertions, 1 failure (the blocked assertion), 0 errors. kondo 0/0.

